#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" :question/math-academy-id "e-9907" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA$, find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3131/Source/Images/ba65b415e5958ff6ec13493e6641c014.png)" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1$,\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_1 \\mid \\mid = 8 = \\sigma_1$, which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64$, and\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_2 \\mid \\mid = 6 = \\sigma_2$, which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36$.\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = 36\n$$\n\nof $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=2$, and $\\sigma_3 = 0$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A$. Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4$, the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91474655-212b-55f0-b086-d5578984f699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159259} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b90e3d-bf69-5b8c-9214-a72ea025c69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159260} {:answer/id #uuid "08277399-e698-5fa3-b0f6-be9ce2be8dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159261} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3eff44-c5d5-58e0-bce9-ce8d2b9b322c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159262} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0777fb-f320-5948-85f4-e21a9e448196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08277399-e698-5fa3-b0f6-be9ce2be8dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159261} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fc5061-2971-509d-ae61-dd733bd377be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159258}] :question/id #uuid "b2e59ffd-322c-5739-bff6-4abbfbcffef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139415" :question/problem "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $5$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e082731-db2e-5189-a6e9-b8b1ddaa147f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159265} {:answer/id #uuid "e4076dfa-7378-5413-949b-6eee7814eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159266} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ad6ca8-07de-5d49-9a65-e5f5986dda2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159267} {:answer/id #uuid "210eafc0-0495-5eda-8a5e-427af20603f